Z||000000||cmyokley||11-05-1999||11:36 AM||||IS IT POSIBLE TO OVER PRESSURE THE FUEL RAIL? gOING TO WORK TODAY IN MY 86GT I STOPPED TO DROP OFF SOME DRY CLEANING. WHEN I CLIMED BACK INTO MY CAR IT WOULD CRANK BUT NOT FIRE OVER. SINCE IT IS A NEW RE-BUILD WITH LESS THAN 500 MILES ON IT I WAS NOT TOO SUPPRISED TO HAVE SOME KIND OF PROBLEM...BUT... SINCE IT WAS TURNING OVER I ASSUMED THAT IT WAS NOT GETTING FIRE OR FUEL. I CHECKED THE WIRES, DETERMINED THAT EVERYTHING WAS IN PLACE. SO I TURNED TO THE FUEL. I CHECKED HOSES AND ALL SEEMED IN ORDER. (ALL THE TIME I AM TRYING TO CRANK THE CAN BUT IT JUST SPINS). I THEN REMOVED THE CAP FOR THE SCHRADER VALVE AND RELIEVED A LITTLE PRESSURE FROM THE RAIL...THE CAR THEN FIRED UP LIKE IT SHOULD.<P>WHAT IS THE DEAL...IS IT POSSIBLE TO OVER PRESSURE THE FUEL RAIL. AND IF THIS IS THE CASE WHAT IS THE PROBLEM...THE FUEL REGULATOR?<P>ANY IDEAS.||||reg||8
Z||000001||theogre||11-05-1999||08:43 PM||||did you replace the fuel reg? is it one of the adjustable ones? It may be posible that the thing is set to high.<P>I've seen this V6 in other cars do this. Never did get a good answer from GM or any of their so called factory trained goodwrench heads as to why. It never seemed to happen with any regularity which made it even more annoying.<P>Did you get liquid immediatly or an air bubble first? could be you vapor locked it. when you take a fuel system apart on an engine like these V6 sometimes it takes a couple manual bleeds to get rid of all the air. they don't always get rid of all of it by themselves. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||WKDFIRO||11-05-1999||09:24 PM||||Ive NEVER heard of a V6 being over preasured. The fuel injectors have a set volume no matter what the fuel pump is doing, (unless it is low.). What you are describing sounds like ignition. When you got the rebuild did you replace the pickup coil, module and coil? When they go bad they act up at the slightest heat source and act like nothing happened if they sit and cool off, like when you are checking the fuel rail....||||reg||1
Z||000003||cmyokley||11-08-1999||09:10 AM||||Theorge...My fuel regulator is one that is integral to the fuel rail (I think). I was under the assunption that one would have to replace the complete rail if the regulator went bad. I do not think there is air in the system, but not sure I will re-check the pressure this week sometime to see if it has gone up or down since I installed the new pump.<P>WKDFIRO...when I rebuilt the 2.8 I rebuilt the dist., replaced the pickup coil, module etc. Then I replaced the coil with an MSD 6A and MSD coil (GM style). This weekend I started wondering if this MSD is shorting.<P>What is most disturbing is that the problem has no pattern. <P>How do I check the MSD? What should I do when this happens to me again? Any comments.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||GWMORGAN||11-08-1999||11:31 AM||||If the MSD box is not connected properly,then the plugs won't fire as they should, resulting in no starts or poor performance (I had the same problem). Try bypassing the MSD and see how she runs, then take it from there.||||reg||1
